     ❱ tools.opengrep.qnxml-ftruncate-resizes-shared-object
          ftruncate on a shm_open fd with O_CREAT but without O_EXCL: if the shared object already exists
          (e.g. a previous run or another participant sized it differently), this silently resizes shared
          state other processes may be using. Consider O_EXCL for create, or fstat and verify the existing
          size first.

           65┆ if (Bool_val(vcreate) && ftruncate(fd, (off_t) bytes) != 0) {

    ❯❱ tools.opengrep.qnxml-map-fixed-silent-overwrite
          MAP_FIXED silently replaces any existing mapping overlapping the requested range — a wrong hint
          address can destroy the process's own address space with no error. Prefer MAP_FIXED_NOREPLACE (check
          EEXIST) or verify the range is free before mapping.

           70┆ if (hint != NULL) flags |= MAP_FIXED;

    ❯❱ home.jm.data.qnx..opengrep-rules.semgrep-rules.c.lang.security.insecure-use-memset
          When handling sensitive information in a buffer, it's important to ensure  that the data is securely
          erased before the buffer is deleted or reused.  While `memset()` is commonly used for this purpose,
          it can leave sensitive  information behind due to compiler optimizations or other factors.  To avoid
          this potential vulnerability, it's recommended to use the  `memset_s()` function instead.
          `memset_s()` is a standardized function  that securely overwrites the memory with a specified value,
          making it more  difficult for an attacker to recover any sensitive data that was stored in  the
          buffer. By using `memset_s()` instead of `memset()`, you can help to  ensure that your application
          is more secure and less vulnerable to exploits  that rely on residual data in memory.

           ▶▶┆ Autofix ▶ memset_s(p, 0, 0)
           77┆ memset(p, 0, 0);  /* keep contents; pre-fault read/write below */

    ❯❱ tools.opengrep.qnxml-msgsendv-reply-discarded
          MsgSendv collects the reply into a stack-local buffer that is never read — the reply payload is
          silently discarded (the comment suggests a caller buffer). Either return the reply words to OCaml or
          shrink the reply iov to zero and document that sends are ack-only.

          207┆ rc = MsgSendv(Long_val(vcoid), &siov, 1, &riov, 1);

    ❯❱ tools.opengrep.qnxml-pulse-eagain-crashes-producer
          MsgSendPulse fails with EAGAIN when the receiver's pulse pool is exhausted; failing hard turns
          backpressure (producer spin-waiting on a full ring, pulsing every retry) into a producer crash.
          Return the error to OCaml and let the caller rate-limit or coalesce wakeups.

          280┆ if (rc < 0) caml_failwith("MsgSendPulse");

