     ❱ tools.opengrep.qnxml-int64-boxing-in-steady-state
          Int64.of_int allocates a boxed int64 on the OCaml heap. Inside push/pop/tick hot paths this
          contradicts the "zero OCaml heap allocation in steady state" contract (each boxing is a GC-visible
          minor allocation). Audit: keep boxing to init-only paths, or accept and document the minor-
          allocation rate.

           65┆ Region.store_rel hdr 4 (Int64.of_int chid);
            ⋮┆----------------------------------------
           66┆ Region.store_rel hdr 3 (Int64.of_int (Unix.getpid ()));

    ❯❱ tools.opengrep.qnxml-shared-counter-truncation
          A shared 64-bit counter is truncated to a 63-bit OCaml int. The monotone-never-wrap invariant holds
          for int64, but arithmetic after to_int wraps at 2^62; OCaml's (mod) then goes negative and indexing
          raises. Fine for realistic lifetimes, but the "never wrapped" claim in the docs must be qualified to
          63 bits on the OCaml side.

           68┆ Region.connect_attach (Int64.to_int (Region.load_acq hdr 1))
            ⋮┆----------------------------------------
           69┆ (Int64.to_int (Region.load_acq hdr 2))

